12. Model selection — How do you choose an LLM?
A GenAI Engineer should not choose a model only because it is popular.

The correct choice depends on the task and the application's constraints.

| Factor | Questions to ask |
|---|---|
| Quality | Does the model perform well on our actual tasks? |
| Latency | How quickly does it respond? |
| Cost | What are the inference or hosting costs? |
| Context window | Can it handle the required input and output sizes? |
| Privacy | Can the data be sent to the provider, or must the model run in a controlled environment? |
| Tool support | Can it reliably support the required tool-calling workflow? |
| Structured output | Can it produce the required format reliably? |
| Deployment | Is hosted API access or self-hosting more appropriate? |
| Evaluation | How does it perform on representative test cases? |


Banking example
Suppose you're building two applications.

Application A: Simple ticket classification

You may be able to use a smaller model or even a traditional classifier, depending on accuracy requirements.

Application B: Complex document analysis

A more capable model with suitable context capacity may be needed, potentially combined with document retrieval.

The best choice is the model that meets the requirements at an acceptable cost and operational complexity.

Interview answer:
"I select an LLM based on task-specific evaluation, response quality, latency, cost, context capacity, privacy, deployment constraints, and tool support. I compare candidate models using representative test cases instead of relying only on benchmark rankings."

